'' '-exx' CHECKS A DEREFERENCE FOR NULL. fb_NullPtrChk( p, line, filename ), the
'' shape measured from fbc's own IR; the abort it produces is runtime error 7 with
'' exit code 7, matched against fbc on the same program.
''
'' TWO CALLS FOR THE TWO DEREFERENCES, and that is the point of where the check
'' sits. '*p = 9' lowers to LowerAddr then a store, and 'print *p' to LowerAddr then
'' a load -- so the check goes in LowerAddr, once, rather than at the read and the
'' write separately where the two could drift apart.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -exx --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/nullchk.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: call ptr @"fb_NullPtrChk"
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare ptr @"fb_NullPtrChk"

'' Taking an address is not a dereference and is not checked: '@v' is legal on
'' anything, and a check there would fire on code that is correct.
'' EXPECT-NOT: fb_ArraySngBoundChkEx
